  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1245 AM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nUpper level ridging shifts west to southern CA Tuesday as a weak ill \ndefined shortwave moves east from the Great Basin to the Four \nCorners. This shortwave will result in a few light showers and an \nisolated storm or two across the north central mountains Tuesday \nafternoon. The shortwave/ill-defined low moves south over the state \nWednesday with it's axis horizontally oriented from east to west. \nDaytime heating under the shortwave axis will result in a few \nisolated showers and storms across the higher terrain Wednesday \nafternoon. This disturbance looks to close off into a more defined \nupper low over southern NM and far West Texas Thursday. \nPrecipitation associated with the upper low looks to remain mostly \nsouth of the CWA at this time with some isolated showers and storms \nacross the Sacramento Mountains and southern Chaves County Thursday \nafternoon. Deterministic and ensemble guidance has the upper low \nmeandering east-northeast to the TX Big Bend and Permian Basin \nFriday resulting in some low chances for showers and storms across \nsoutheast NM Friday afternoon. That upper low opens up and exits \neast next weekend with southwest flow over the state in advance of a \nnew upper level trough over the Pacific coast and Great Basin. High \ntemperatures will be in the low to mid 70s for lower elevations \nacross western and central NM and upper 70s to mid 80s across the \neastern plains Tuesday through Friday. Highs across western areas \ncool slightly into the mid to upper 60s next weekend due to lowering \nheights in advance of the Pacific trough.",
